Implementation note: translating pages

Ladybird splits a page into paragraph units over its rendered content,
ending a paragraph at a block, an inline-block, or a line break, and
keeps a paragraph’s inline elements with tokens the translation carries
over to the translated words. WebKit’s TextManipulationController does
the same, driven by Safari’s WBSTranslationWebpageContentExtractor: Its
ParagraphContentIterator and isEnclosingItemBoundaryElement() split
rendered text into paragraph items of identified tokens, and its
observeParagraphs() reaches into author shadow roots but not user-agent
ones, as Ladybird’s scan does.

Rebuilding a paragraph follows WebKit’s updateInsertions(): The first
use of an element is the element itself, and a second use is a shallow
clone. WebKit also copies the event listeners not created from markup
onto the clone, with copyEventListenersNotCreatedFromMarkupToTarget();
Ladybird doesn’t yet. Gecko’s TranslationsDocument sends each subtree
that has no block-like descendants as HTML with temporary IDs (by its
getIsBlockLike() and nodeNeedsSubdividing()), and its updateElement()
deep-clones a repeated element instead.

The exclusions follow Gecko’s CONTENT_EXCLUDED_TAGS for code, kbd, samp,
and var, its handling of translate=no and the “notranslate” class, and
its exclusion of editable content and of content declared in another
language; WebKit leaves exclusions to its client. The attributes are the
ones in WebKit’s isAttributeForTextManipulation(), and Gecko’s rule in
TRANSLATABLE_ATTRIBUTES that a button’s value is translated but a submit
button’s isn’t applies too — except to a submit button with no name,
whose value isn’t submitted. Gecko’s updateElement() also keeps the
text of an option as its value before translating it, as Ladybird does.

Translating the text in the viewport first, and hidden text last,
matches Gecko’s intersection-observer priorities; WebKit supplies the
geometry, and Safari’s scheduling isn’t in the source. The root’s lang
attribute becomes the target language, as it does in Gecko, which sets
document.documentElement.lang, and in Safari, whose extractor has
_updateHTMLLanguageAttributesIfNeeded:. Showing the original reloads the
page, as it does in Gecko (TranslationsParent.restorePage()) and Safari
(WBSTranslationContext’s reloadPageInOriginalLanguage). Continuing on
the next page of the same site in the same language matches Safari’s
isContinuedTranslationEnabled and _previousPageHighLevelDomain, while
Chromium’s LanguageState::InTranslateNavigation() continues on any link
the user follows to a page in the same language, whatever its site.

The language sample is up to 4,096 UTF-16 code units of the text in the
page’s tree, the length of Gecko’s #TEXT_SAMPLE_TARGET_CODE_UNITS. But
Gecko samples the rendered text, and Ladybird the text in the tree, so
taking the sample never lays the page out. A detection counts only with
at least 25 characters of text and a probability above 0.7: Chromium’s
kMinimumContentLengthBytes and kTFLiteReliabilityThreshold. Chromium’s
page translation itself runs in a script it downloads, so how it
extracts and rebuilds text isn’t in its source.
